I've characterized Kripke's three main build/execution variants based on solid source evidence:

**Subject**: `kripke.exe` — a single executable built via CMake+BLT superbuild (Kripke is a 3D Sn deterministic particle-transport mini-app / proxy for LLNL's ARDRA code).

**Variants recorded:**

1. **`cpu-mpi-openmp`** — Sequential or OpenMP on-node kernels, MPI Cartesian domain decomposition (`--procs px,py,pz`), asynchronous `MPI_Isend`/`MPI_Irecv` neighbor-exchange sweep (I/J/K boundary planes) plus `MPI_Allreduce` for global sums. Latency-sensitive neighbor communication, no checkpoint/parallel I/O.

2. **`cuda-gpu-mpi`** — Same MPI sweep algorithm, but on-node kernels use RAJA `cuda_exec`; optional `ENABLE_GPU_AWARE_MPI` (requires CHAI) passes device pointers directly into MPI buffers instead of staging through host memory — wants GPU-direct-capable MPI and rank/GPU co-location.

3. **`hip-gpu-mpi`** — Same architecture as CUDA variant but on AMD ROCm/HIP GPUs (MI100/MI250X/MI300A host-configs); one host-config even disables MPI, suggesting single-GPU/single-node runs are also supported.

All three share: neighbor-pattern point-to-point sweep communication (the dominant, latency-sensitive traffic), periodic allreduce for solver residual/particle-balance checks, no checkpoint/restart, no parallel I/O library usage, and a Cartesian process-grid launch geometry chosen via `--procs`/`--zset` at runtime rather than hardcoded counts.
